"""
Perfect 5-Act Narrative & Spoken Script for EN_EP0008_oakland_plan_rejected.
Macro Audit Council Remediations (Opus 5.5 & Astra 6 Round 6 Master Polish):
- N1: Exact Plan Identity - Third Amended Plan (Docket 1204, $143.5M trust, March 17, 2025) explicitly identified as the sole operative plan of record denied confirmation on October 5, 2026. Clarifies that the December 2025 $242M mediation term sheet never matured into a Fourth Amended Plan.
- N2: Stay law precision - denial of confirmation leaves dismissal motion active; automatic stay faces potential dissolution if court grants dismissal or stay relief.
- N3: Statutory priority & fees - plain language priority for court-approved professional fees; clarifies cumulative fee applications surpassed $44.6M with tens of millions approved and paid on an interim basis ahead of unsecured recovery.
- N4: Operating deficits vs fees - $29M operating deficits across fiscal years clearly separated from $44.6M professional fees.
- N5: Insurance legal precision - historical liability policies are property of estate; non-debtor carriers stood outside bankruptcy seeking releases.
- N6: Section 1129(a)(10) & Purdue distinction - §1129(a)(10) requires accepting impaired class; Purdue (June 27, 2024) bars nonconsensual releases even under cramdown.
- N7: Denominator consistency - 99% of voting survivor creditors; more than 330 claimants filed proofs of claim.
- N8: Dismissal posture abeyance - September 9, 2025 dismissal motion held in abeyance while debtor maintained confirmation request during court-ordered mediation.
- N9: Age anchors & inclusive phrasing - survivors, many now in their late sixties to eighties.
- N10: Full TTS pronunciation normalization - section eleven twenty-nine a ten, between nineteen-sixty and nineteen-eighty-nine, June twenty-seventh, twenty twenty-four.
"""

STORY_TEXT = """=== ACT I ===

How does a multi-year bankruptcy effort—carrying proposals from a $143.5 million trust under its Third Amended Plan to a $242 million final mediation offer—end up rejected by a federal judge without an approved distribution? On October 5, 2026, the bankruptcy court answered that question for hundreds of pending abuse lawsuits.

Public headlines recorded the rejection. Docket entries recorded the case number. Neither explained the machinery. The Oakland Diocese reorganization did not stall over whether dollars could be promised on paper. In December 2025, the diocese formally offered a $242 million settlement fund spread over five years during court-ordered mediation, calling it its last and final position in bankruptcy court. But the headline dollar figures obscured the actual legal machinery: the size of the recoverable fund was strictly constrained by the legal perimeter of the bankruptcy estate. While historical liability insurance policies constituted property of the estate, third-party insurance carriers and parishes stood outside the debtor corporation as separate non-debtor entities, conditioning their financial contributions on sweeping nonconsensual third-party releases shielding them from future litigation. Because parish properties and third-party insurers were held outside the debtor corporation, available estate cash remained limited—creating an insurmountable valuation gap with survivor creditors who viewed the pro-rata offers as inadequate compensation.

On the surface, the proceeding appeared orderly: a plan on file, a claims agent docketing paperwork, parishes open on Sunday, and a trust with a precise figure attached to it. Underneath, the mechanism had seized. Initial schedules listed estate assets of roughly $76.1 million; the larger settlement proposals relied on contested contributions from third-party insurers and parish asset liquidations. Every state court lawsuit sat frozen behind the Chapter 11 automatic stay, leaving pending abuse lawsuits halted without a confirmed settlement plan. The first thing a Chapter 11 filing does is stop the clock on litigation. The only thing it guarantees is that the clock stops for everyone at once. So who was that pause actually protecting? The docket supplies the answer in the professional fee applications on file.

Most of the alleged abuse in these cases occurred between nineteen-sixty and nineteen-eighty-nine. The arithmetic is sobering. A survivor abused at ten years old in nineteen-seventy-two turned sixty-four while case number twenty-three, four-oh-five-two-three passed its third anniversary in the Northern District of California. Three years of motions. Three years of fee applications. Three years in which a claimant in their seventies either lived to see a distribution or did not. The shock of the ruling was not that a judge disliked a number. It was that a process built to end a crisis had made the crisis permanent for the people trapped inside it.

The central challenge was whether a collective trust structure could ever bridge the valuation gap with survivor creditors who viewed the settlement offers as fundamentally insufficient. When the Official Committee of Unsecured Creditors signaled overwhelming opposition to the diocese's proposals, the fault lines were already formed.

=== ACT II ===

Rewind to October 13, 2019. California's governor signs Assembly Bill 218, the Child Victims Act. Effective January 1, 2020, it extends the statute of limitations on childhood sexual abuse claims and revives cases that had been time-barred for decades. Claims that were legally dead became legally alive. By May 8, 2023, more than 330 lawsuits alleging child sexual abuse had surged against the Diocese of Oakland, and the diocese responded by filing a voluntary Chapter 11 petition before Judge William J. Lafferty. The sequence reveals the strategy: the statute opened the door, and bankruptcy was the institutional answer chosen before a single revived case reached a civil jury.

In coverage from The Oaklandside to CBS San Francisco and in diocesan statements, Chapter 11 was presented as the fair forum: one judge, one pool of assets, one equitable distribution, avoiding a race to the courthouse where early plaintiffs drain the estate and late ones receive nothing. That account is the comforting half. The central tension in Chapter 11 is that it seeks collective resolution: channeling claims into a unified proceeding rather than litigating hundreds of separate jury trials in state court.

Two documented operational shifts shaped the financial reality. Mass attendance across the diocese dropped roughly 42% between 2019 and 2021 during the pandemic and never fully recovered, permanently depressing local parish collections. Because parishes operate as separate legal entities, parish funds sit outside the estate—yet the central diocese depends on monthly cathedraticum assessments remitted from those parish collections. When weekly collections shrank, the flow of diocesan assessment revenues contracted in tandem, directly constraining the central chancery's capacity to fund installment payments for a multi-million-dollar trust. Second, the legal perimeter of the estate itself. Parishes and schools are held separately from the central corporate entity, which means the pile of assets a judge can reach is narrower than the institution a survivor believes they sued. Add insurance policies written in the nineteen-sixties, seventies and eighties, where coverage terms, limits and surviving documentation are contested line by line.

Looking past convenient narratives, the obstacle was structural. The popular version is that plaintiff lawyers wanted too much, or that the diocese simply had no money. Both are myths of comfort. This diocese is a separate legal entity under California law, distinct from the global church, and conflating them obscures where assets legally reside. The obstacle was never simply a headline dollar amount; it was the contested boundary of the estate and the legal impossibility of granting third-party releases to parishes and insurers. What was consuming the estate while the parties fought over those legal boundaries? The administrative machinery of Chapter 11 itself, running the meter while negotiations remained deadlocked.

=== ACT III ===

March 17, 2025. The diocese files its Third Amended Plan of Reorganization, Docket 1204, proposing approximately $143.5 million for a Survivors' Trust. Under this Third Amended Plan, the proposed trust aimed to resolve claims collectively. But survivor advocates consistently criticized the offers as insufficient, and on June 6, 2025, a voting declaration filed by the claims agent and documented by the Official Committee of Unsecured Creditors showed a 99% rejection rate among voting survivor creditors.

Three months later, on September 9, 2025, the diocese moved to dismiss its own Chapter 11 case, citing an insurmountable negotiation impasse after absorbing nearly $29 million in operating deficits across fiscal years. Rather than granting immediate dismissal, the bankruptcy court held the debtor's dismissal motion in abeyance to compel intensive mediation. Crucially, the debtor maintained its underlying request for confirmation of an amended plan on the docket during this period while the dismissal motion sat on hold. That court-ordered mediation produced the December 2025 term sheet carrying the $242 million five-year proposal—the diocese's last and final offer. But that mediation term sheet never matured into a confirmed consensual Fourth Amended Plan. Survivors could not accept a pro-rata share of a capped fund while a jury might value a single claim far higher. The diocese could not fund a number large enough to clear 99% opposition without reaching assets it maintained were never part of the estate. Both sides behaved rationally. The structure produced the deadlock.

Then the ledger spoke, revealing who the pause was truly protecting. By mid-2026, cumulative professional fee applications on file had climbed past $44.6 million—roughly 59% of the estate's total initial scheduled assets of $76.1 million. The $29 million in operating deficits reflected diocesan cash shortfalls across fiscal years, separate from the $44.6 million in professional fee applications that accumulated as administrative priority claims against the estate.

The meter never stopped.

Under Bankruptcy Code statutory priority rules, court-approved professional fees are paid ahead of general unsecured creditors from estate funds. Every fee application is subject to judicial review and creditor objection, yet tens of millions had already been authorized and paid on an interim basis under court orders ahead of any recovery for survivor creditors. The longer confirmation stalls, the smaller the pool that survivors are fighting over. The pause protected the process, and administrative costs continued to mount. In an adversary proceeding complaint, creditors' attorneys alleged that earlier diocesan corporate restructurings transferred approximately $106 million in parish real estate beyond estate reach; that lawsuit resulted in no finding of liability, as the bankruptcy court formally dismissed the adversary action. The warning was the docket itself, filed publicly, month after month. Could this plan legally buy peace for insurers and parishes without claimant consent?

=== ACT IV ===

The structure failed in stages, not at once. While the December 2025 settlement term sheet carrying the $242 million offer attempted to resolve the dispute, it never converted into a confirmed consensual plan. The Third Amended Plan remained the formal operative plan of record on the court's docket. The fundamental divide remained: a jury putting a market price on a single claim. That event arrived on April 22, 2026.

In state court, where stay relief had been granted for a bellwether case, a jury awarded $16 million to a man abused by an East Bay priest, as reported by KQED, with implications for hundreds of pending claims. Consider the stark comparison: one jury verdict, sixteen million dollars. The $143.5 million trust proposal under the Third Amended Plan averaged roughly $435,000 across more than 330 claimants who filed proofs of claim; even the $242 million mediation term sheet averaged about $733,000 per person over five years. A single state-court jury award eclipsed what twenty claimants would receive under the entire collective bankruptcy fund. Bellwether results are not automatic multipliers, and every case carries distinct defenses. But as a negotiating baseline, it reframed everything. That is why "last and final" stopped functioning as a deadline and started functioning as an arbitrary ceiling nobody had agreed to.

The legal grounds for collapse were structural. First, under Bankruptcy Code section eleven twenty-nine a ten, plan confirmation requires acceptance by at least one impaired class of creditors—a statutory threshold impossible to satisfy when 99% of voting survivor creditors rejected the plan. Second, on June twenty-seventh, twenty twenty-four, midway through the bankruptcy, the United States Supreme Court handed down Harrington versus Purdue Pharma, holding that bankruptcy courts lack statutory authority to grant nonconsensual third-party releases shielding non-debtor entities like parishes and insurance carriers over creditor objection. Consensual releases were impossible because survivor creditors voted 99% against the Third Amended Plan. And nonconsensual cramdown under section eleven twenty-nine b could not legally shield the non-debtor affiliates under Supreme Court precedent. When the proposed plan came before the federal judge, the court determined that the collective settlement could not be approved under federal law.

On October 5, 2026, Judge Lafferty entered the order: formal confirmation of the Third Amended Plan of Reorganization was denied. Not a moral verdict on the institution—a judicial ruling that this specific legal instrument could not deliver nonconsensual third-party releases over overwhelming creditor opposition. Across three years, professionals were paid on an interim basis under court authorization from estate assets, totaling $44.6 million, ahead of any recovery for survivor creditors. Meanwhile, the survivors, many now in their late sixties to eighties, absorbed the delay, still waiting for resolution.

=== ACT V ===

Strip it to the causal chain. A 2019 California statute revived time-barred claims. A 2023 voluntary Chapter 11 petition paused them in bankruptcy. Over the following three years, competing reorganization proposals were met with consistent survivor rejection, while professional fees consumed roughly 59% of the estate's initial assets. On October 5, 2026, the federal judge formally rejected the Third Amended Plan of Reorganization. The ruling was procedural, not triumphal—a rejected plan is a rejected instrument, not a closed case and not a payout. Nothing was distributed on October 5, 2026. The ledger stayed frozen.

With confirmation of the Third Amended Plan definitively denied and the debtor's September 2025 dismissal motion returning to active consideration on the docket, the protective wall of the automatic stay faced potential dissolution. If the bankruptcy court enters an order granting the pending dismissal motion or granting stay relief, the automatic stay dissolves—freeing survivors from bankruptcy constraints to pursue individual lawsuits in California state court, where that $16 million bellwether now sits in the record. That path carries both opportunities and risks: individual trials take years, results vary widely, and the diocese's financial strain from depleted parish assessments will remain a factor in post-bankruptcy collections. Some claimants will recover more than a trust share. Some will recover later. Some, given the timeline reaching back to nineteen-sixty, will not be alive for either outcome.

The broader lesson extends far beyond one diocese in the East Bay. Any institution facing hundreds of aggregated legacy claims faces the same arithmetic: file, stay the litigation, cap the exposure, and attempt to buy protection for entities and insurers standing just outside the estate. After Purdue Pharma, that shield no longer works by decree. Filers attempt consent solicitation, narrowed estate definitions, and extended payment tails. The warning for anyone tracking mass-claim bankruptcies today is not that the courts failed. It is that time itself is a transferable asset, and in this case it moved in one direction only.

Returning to the question posed at the start: how does a multi-year Chapter 11 process end without an approved distribution? Here is the record's answer. It was a contested reorganization effort—the Third Amended Plan was rejected by 99% of voting survivor creditors, weighed down by mounting administrative expenses, and formally denied confirmation by a federal judge on October 5, 2026. The proposed settlement could not deliver nonconsensual releases under Supreme Court precedent. The clock was the real cost. Legal arguments and plan proposals can be redrafted for years, while the claimants waiting for resolution in state court are the one constituency whose time cannot be restored."""

